  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 333 AM EDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis and Forecast: \n\nA midlevel split flow-pattern begins to build across North America \nthis morning with ridging over the Northern Plains while low \namplitude troughing spans the southern U.S. Midlevel ridging will \nsupport subsidence aloft this morning and cause surface high \npressure to build across the Great Lakes region through the next \nseveral days. H8 temperatures in the single digits will keep \ntemperatures seasonably normal through the entirety of the forecast \nperiod with highs in the 60s and overnight lows in the upper 30s to \nlow 40s. \n\n\nCloud cover will continue to dissipate and progress south this \nmorning as the stationary boundary currently occupying the \nAppalachians moves downstream. High pressure building over central \nOntario will keep conditions quiet and dry over the CWA throughout \nthe entirety of the forecast period. Efficient overnight radiational \ncooling will drop lows into the mid/upper 30s across interior \neastern upper and northern lower leading to another round of patchy \nfrost development tonight. Otherwise, conditions remain \nclimatologically normal for late September with highs in the 60s for \nmost areas while efficient overnight radiational cooling processes \ndrop lows into the upper 30s to low 40s. Other than ideal fall \nconditions for hardwood color development, no out-of-the-ordinary or \nactive weather is expected at this time. Long-range ensembles \ncontinue to hint at low-end chances of showers midweek next week as \na shortwave lifts across the Northern Plains, but confidence in any \nappreciable (if any) rainfall reaching the Great Lakes Region \nremains low.",
